<span id="private-title" class="anchor-alias" aria-hidden="true"></span><span
  id="private-close-title"
  class="anchor-alias"
  aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section
  class="feature-copy-grid feature-copy-grid--top"
  aria-labelledby="private-behavior-title"
>
  <div>
    <h2
      id="private-behavior-title"
    >
      Use a private tab, then leave no history behind.
    </h2>
  </div>
  <div class="feature-copy-list">
    <article>
      <h3>Not in history.</h3><p>
        Pages you visit in a private tab stay out of Blanc's browsing history,
        so they do not become part of the local record.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Not waiting after a restart.</h3><p>
        Private tabs are excluded from session restore and reopen-closed. They
        do not quietly return the next time you open Blanc.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>One simple exit.</h3><p>
        The private chip on the island is a quick way to close the active
        private tab when you are finished.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
<aside class="truth-note" aria-labelledby="private-note-title">
  <h2 id="private-note-title">
    Private does not mean anonymous.
  </h2><p>
    Private tabs share one separate in-memory browsing session with other
    private tabs opened during the same Blanc run; they do not share cookies or
    site storage with regular tabs, and the private session is discarded when
    Blanc quits. Websites, downloads, your network, or an employer may still
    observe activity, and files you explicitly save remain on disk.
  </p><a
    class="text-link"
    href="/privacy#what-stays-on-your-device"
    data-track="feature_cta_click"
    data-feature="private-tabs"
    data-cta-position="truth-note"
    >Read the full privacy policy <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
</aside>
